<!--
  Preserves the Haiven.Sdk assembly (this package's Runtime asmdef: WebGlSocket,
  UnityHandoff) from IL2CPP managed stripping.

  REMOVAL EXPERIMENT, 2026-10-11: unity-editor.yml run 38113269264 on a probe
  branch (main d185fd9 with only this file deleted) passed every step on Unity
  2022.3.62f2 with High managed stripping: EditMode, PlayMode, the WebGL build,
  and the Chromium smoke (HAIVEN_WEBGL_PASS, a Unicode socket round trip,
  protocol parsing, disposal, and the handoff exchange). So the smoke does not
  need this file. What that does and does not establish:

    - The JavaScript callbacks are NOT stripped without it. They carry
      [MonoPInvokeCallback], which IL2CPP keeps; inbound frames arrived in the
      stripped player. That half of the original rationale was wrong.

    - Typed JSON decoding was NOT covered by the experiment. The built-in codec
      now calls generated readers and writers directly, and `make check-aot`
      proves the portable core has zero IL2xxx/IL3xxx warnings without a
      baseline allowance. The opt-in System.Text.Json adapter is outside that
      default AOT claim.

  Haiven.Sdk.Core is deliberately not preserved. BuiltInJsonCodec and the
  generated wire readers use direct calls rather than reflection, so ordinary
  managed reachability is both sufficient and a more truthful stripping rule.

  Kept because it costs nothing and validate-package.mjs expects exactly one,
  not because a build was observed to need it.
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="Haiven.Sdk" preserve="all" />
</linker>
